></path></svg></span>Check out our tutorial Focused on Slurm</div><div clas=
s=3D"admonitionContent_BuS1"><p>If you are new to Hyak and using the job sc=
heduler, Slurm, you may find our Slurm tutorial helpful to walk you through=
 basic and advanced usage. <a href=3D"https://hyak.uw.edu/docs/hyak101/basi=
cs/syllabus_slurm" target=3D"_blank" rel=3D"noopener noreferrer"><strong>Cl=
ick here to jump to the tutorial.</strong></a>
This tutorial can also be followed in video form <a href=3D"https://www.you=
tube.com/watch?v=3DiYM7xpRhp8I" target=3D"_blank" rel=3D"noopener noreferre=
r"><strong>HERE</strong></a>.</p></div></div>
<h2 class=3D"anchor anchorWithStickyNavbar_LWe7" id=3D"compute-resources">C=
ompute Resources<a href=3D"https://hyak.uw.edu/docs/compute/scheduling-jobs=
#compute-resources" class=3D"hash-link" aria-label=3D"Direct link to Comput=
e Resources" title=3D"Direct link to Compute Resources">=E2=80=8B</a></h2>
<p>The Slurm scheduler has two high-level concepts you need to know, <a hre=
f=3D"https://hyak.uw.edu/docs/compute/start-here#accounts" target=3D"_blank=
" rel=3D"noopener noreferrer"><strong>accounts</strong></a> and <a href=3D"=
https://hyak.uw.edu/docs/compute/start-here#partitions" target=3D"_blank" r=
el=3D"noopener noreferrer"><strong>partitions</strong></a>.</p>
<p>With the <code>hyakalloc</code> command <a href=3D"https://hyak.uw.edu/d=
ocs/compute/resource-monitoring#hyakalloc"><strong>source code here</strong=
></a> you can further see not only which accounts you are able to submit jo=
bs to but also their current utilization. Resource limits are directly prop=
ortional to what was contributed by that group.</p>
<p>While you won't necessarily have access to them, it might be useful for =
you to see a list of Hyak's partitions. The <code>sinfo</code> commands con=
tains information about the servers or nodes that compose Hyak, and the <co=
de>sinfo -s</code> commands give you a summary for this information includi=
ng the partitions and the hostnames that fall into each partition.</p>
