<p>Each partition represents a class of node from the standard <code>comput=
e</code> partition to those with high-memory or for different types of GPUs=
.</p>
<h2 class=3D"anchor anchorWithStickyNavbar_LWe7" id=3D"job-types">Job Types=
<a href=3D"https://hyak.uw.edu/docs/compute/scheduling-jobs#job-types" clas=
s=3D"hash-link" aria-label=3D"Direct link to Job Types" title=3D"Direct lin=
k to Job Types">=E2=80=8B</a></h2>
<p>There are a few popular types of jobs you could submit:</p>
<ul>
<li><a href=3D"https://hyak.uw.edu/docs/compute/scheduling-jobs#interactive=
-jobs"><strong>interactive</strong></a> where you and test out your workflo=
ws live,</li>
<li><a href=3D"https://hyak.uw.edu/docs/compute/scheduling-jobs#batch-jobs"=
><strong>batch</strong></a> which are unattended (you get an email when com=
pleted), and</li>
<li><a href=3D"https://hyak.uw.edu/docs/compute/scheduling-jobs#null"><stro=
ng>recurring</strong></a> or "CRON-like" processes that happen on a regular=
 basis.</li>
</ul>
<h3 class=3D"anchor anchorWithStickyNavbar_LWe7" id=3D"slurm-arguments">Slu=
rm Arguments<a href=3D"https://hyak.uw.edu/docs/compute/scheduling-jobs#slu=
rm-arguments" class=3D"hash-link" aria-label=3D"Direct link to Slurm Argume=
nts" title=3D"Direct link to Slurm Arguments">=E2=80=8B</a></h3>
<p>These are the common and recommended arguments suggested at a minimum to=
 get a job in any form.</p>
<div class=3D"theme-admonition theme-admonition-important admonition_xJq3 a=
lert alert--info"><div class=3D"admonitionHeading_Gvgb"><span class=3D"admo=
nitionIcon_Rf37"><svg viewBox=3D"0 0 14 16"><path fill-rule=3D"evenodd" d=
=3D"M7 2.3c3.14 0 5.7 2.56 5.7 5.7s-2.56 5.7-5.7 5.7A5.71 5.71 0 0 1 1.3 8c=
0-3.14 2.56-5.7 5.7-5.7zM7 1C3.14 1 0 4.14 0 8s3.14 7 7 7 7-3.14 7-7-3.14-7=
-7-7zm1 3H6v5h2V4zm0 6H6v2h2v-2z"></path></svg></span>important</div><div c=
lass=3D"admonitionContent_BuS1"><p>If you are using an interactive node to =
run a parallel application such as Python multiprocessing, MPI, OpenMP, etc=
. then the number given for the <code>--ntasks-per-node</code> option must =
match the number of processes used by your application.</p></div></div>
<table><thead><tr><th>Arguments</th><th>Command Flags</th><th>Notes</th></t=
r></thead><tbody><tr><td>Account</td><td><code>-A</code> or <code>--account=
</code></td><td>What lab are you part of? If you run the <code>groups</code=
> command you can see what groups (usually labs) you're a member of, these =
are associated with resource limits on the cluster. See the <a href=3D"http=
s://hyak.uw.edu/docs/compute/scheduling-jobs#accounts"><strong>accounts</st=
rong></a> section for additional information.</td></tr><tr><td>Partition</t=
d><td><code>-p</code> or <code>--partition</code></td><td>What resource par=
tition are you interested in using? This could be anything you see when you=
 run <code>sinfo -s</code> as each partition corresponds to a class of node=
s (e.g., high memory, GPU). See the <a href=3D"https://hyak.uw.edu/docs/com=
pute/scheduling-jobs#partitions"><strong>partitions</strong></a> section fo=
r additional information.</td></tr><tr><td>Nodes</td><td><code>-N</code> or=
 <code>--nodes</code></td><td>How many nodes are these resources spread acr=
oss? In the overwhelming number of cases this is 1 (for a single node) but =
more sophisticated multi-node jobs could be run if your code supports it.</=
td></tr><tr><td>Cores</td><td><code>-c</code> or <code>--cpus-per-task</cod=
e></td><td>How many compute cores do you need? Not all codes can make use o=
f multiple cores and if they do, the performance of the code is not always =
linear with the resources requested. If in doubt consider contacting the re=
search computing team to assist in this optimization.</td></tr><tr><td>Memo=
ry</td><td><code>--mem</code></td><td>How much memory do you need for this =
job? This is in the format <code>size[units]</code> were size is a number a=
nd units are either <code>M</code>, <code>G</code>, or <code>T</code> for m=
egabyte, gigabyte, and terabyte respectively. Megabyte is the default unit =
if none is provided.</td></tr><tr><td>Time</td><td><code>-t</code> or <code=
>--time</code></td><td>What's the maximum runtime for this job? Common acce=
ptable time formats include <code>hours:minutes:seconds</code>, <code>days-=
hours</code>, and <code>minutes</code>.</td></tr></tbody></table>
<h3 class=3D"anchor anchorWithStickyNavbar_LWe7" id=3D"interactive-jobs-sin=
gle-node">Interactive Jobs (Single Node)<a href=3D"https://hyak.uw.edu/docs=
/compute/scheduling-jobs#interactive-jobs-single-node" class=3D"hash-link" =
aria-label=3D"Direct link to Interactive Jobs (Single Node)" title=3D"Direc=
t link to Interactive Jobs (Single Node)">=E2=80=8B</a></h3>
<p>Resources for interactive jobs are attained either using <code>salloc</c=
ode>. To request a compute node from the Checkpoint all partition (<code>ck=
pt-all</code>) interactively consider the example below.</p>
<div class=3D"language-shell codeBlockContainer_APcc theme-code-block" styl=
